{:edb/db-before-t 1817 :edb/db-after-t 1818 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535130 50 #inst "2026-10-08T17:37:24.832Z" 13194139535130 true] [17592186133576 1100 "If $D$ is the finite region enclosed by the curves $y=\\sqrt{x-2}$ and $y=1-x$ from $y=0$ to $y=1$, as shown below, then what is $\\displaystyle \\iint\\limits_D (x^2y+xy^2) \\, \\mathrm{d}A$ expressed as a repeated integral?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2152/Source/Images/77c8f86821d718dbfe450f51148a59e3.png)" 13194139535130 false] [17592186133576 1100 "If $D$ is the finite region enclosed by the curves $y=\\sqrt{x-2}$ and $y=1-x$ from $y=0$ to $y=1,$ as shown below, then what is $\\displaystyle \\iint\\limits_D (x^2y+xy^2) \\, \\mathrm{d}A$ expressed as a repeated integral?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055472/assets/0e4cf57408f9ca18dc4613317b8b9bc8b3149da0bf273ad03a8a8a48a6e6f454.png)" 13194139535130 true] [17592186133576 1145 "First, let's represent $D$ as a type II region. Writing\n\n$$\ny=\\sqrt{x-2}\n$$\n\nand $y=1-x$ in terms of $y$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}y = \\sqrt{x - 2} & ⟹x = y^{2} + 2 \\\\ y = 1 - x & ⟹x = 1 - y\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTo determine the left and right functions, we draw a horizontal arrow from left to right through the region, as usual.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2152/Source/Images/6bcc7bf82e91ec4c15377d04f25681af.png)\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\nD = \\bigg\\{(x,y) \\:: \\: 0 \\leq y \\leq 1, \\quad 1-y \\leq x \\leq y^2+2 \\bigg\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∬_(D)(x^{2}y + xy^{2})dA &= ∫_{0}^{1}∫_{1 - y}^{y^{2} + 2}(x^{2}y + xy^{2})dxdy\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535130 false] [17592186133576 1145 "First, let's represent $D$ as a type II region. Writing $y=\\sqrt{x-2}$ and $y=1-x$ in terms of $y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y=\\sqrt{x-2} & \\,⟹\\,x={y}^{2}+2 \\\\ y=1-x & \\,⟹\\,x=1-y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine the left and right functions, we draw a horizontal arrow from left to right through the region, as usual.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055472/assets/f0e531c612c84aa0260b318aa1fbe21c9089270b8c4a1079e08583b7fc5a5d90.png)\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\nD = \\bigg\\{ (x,y) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq y \\leq 1, \\quad 1-y \\leq x  \\leq y^2+2 \\bigg\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}({x}^{2}y+x{y}^{2})\\,dA & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{1-y}^{{y}^{2}+2}({x}^{2}y+x{y}^{2})\\,dx\\,dy\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535130 true] [17592186133577 1100 "Consider the finite region $D$ in the $xy$-plane enclosed between the curves $y=x^2$ and $y=3-2x$ below the line $y=3$. What is $\\displaystyle \\iint\\limits_{D} f\\left({x,y} \\right) \\, \\mathrm{d}A\\:$ expressed as a repeated integral?" 13194139535130 false] [17592186133577 1100 "Consider the finite region $D$ in the $xy$-plane enclosed between the curves $y=x^2$ and $y=3-2x$ below the line $y=3.$ What is $\\displaystyle \\iint\\limits_{D} f\\left( {x,y} \\right) \\, \\mathrm{d}A\\:$ expressed as a repeated integral?" 13194139535130 true] [17592186133577 1145 "First, let's make a sketch of our region.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055472/assets/ffbe0db1ae1bb082e63b0d97324b43dd78334dfbd1e88aaa0744be039dd0efc6.png)\n\nWe need to represent $D$ as a type II region. Writing $y=x^2$ and $y=3-2x$ in terms of $y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y={x}^{2} & \\,⟹\\,x=\\sqrt{y} \\\\ y=3-2x & \\,⟹\\,x=\\frac{3-y}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's determine the limits of integration.\n\n- To determine the $y$-limits, we need to find the $y$-coordinates of the points at which the curves intersect. So, we set the following equation and solve it for $y\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{y} & =\\frac{3-y}{2} \\\\ 4y & =(3-y)^{2} \\\\ 4y & =9-6y+{y}^{2} \\\\ {y}^{2}-10y+9 & =0 \\\\ (y-1)(y-9) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, since our region lies below the line $y=3,$ the $y$-limits are \n\n$$\n1 \\le y \\le 3.\n$$\n\n- To determine the left and right functions, we draw a horizontal arrow from left to right through the region, as usual.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055472/assets/3c56798a33ee2da6d928174b67546945be4bf3594d15e89f3ca39119dd4e2c62.png)\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\nD = \\big\\{ (x,y) \\: : \\: 1 \\leq y \\leq 3, \\quad \\dfrac{3-y}{2} \\leq x  \\leq \\sqrt{y} \\big\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}{\\int }_{(3-y)/2}^{\\sqrt{y}}f(x,y)\\,dx\\,dy\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535130 true] [17592186133577 1145 "First, let's make a sketch of our region.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2152/Source/Images/1c78772e8e145e3e09dbc2d763e7f1cb.png)\n\nWe need to represent $D$ as a type II region. Writing $y=x^2$ and $y=3-2x$ in terms of $y$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}y = x^{2} & ⟹x = \\sqrt{y} \\\\ y = 3 - 2x & ⟹x = \\frac{3 - y}{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNow, let's determine the limits of integration.\n\n- To determine the $y$-limits, we need to find the $y$-coordinates of the points at which the curves intersect. So, we set the following equation and solve it for $y$:\n$\\sqrt{y}|= \\frac{3 - y}{2}; 4y|= (3 - y)^{2}; 4y|= 9 - 6y + y^{2}; y^{2} - 10y + 9|= 0; (y - 1)(y - 9)|= 0$So, since our region lies below the line $y=3$, the $y$-limits are\n$1 \\le y \\le 3$.\n- To determine the left and right functions, we draw a horizontal arrow from left to right through the region, as usual.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2152/Source/Images/d3c6291cf3fb20c734e14cf5a74027de.png)\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\nD = \\big\\{(x,y) \\:: \\: 1 \\leq y \\leq 3, \\quad \\dfrac{3-y}{2} \\leq x \\leq \\sqrt{y} \\big\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∬_(D)f(x, y)dA &= ∫_{1}^{3}∫_{(3 - y)/2}^{\\sqrt{y}}f(x, y)dxdy\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535130 false] [17592186133578 1100 "Calculate $\\displaystyle \\int_{\\sqrt{\\pi}}^{\\sqrt{2\\pi}} \\int_0^{y^2} \\frac{\\sin y^2}{y} \\, \\mathrm{d}x \\,\\mathrm{d}y$." 13194139535130 false] [17592186133578 1100 "Calculate $\\displaystyle \\int_{\\sqrt{\\pi}}^{\\sqrt{2\\pi}} \\int_0^{y^2} \\frac{\\sin y^2}{y} \\, \\mathrm{d}x \\,\\mathrm{d}y.$" 13194139535130 true] [17592186133578 1145 "The integral is defined over a type II region.\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $x$, treating $y$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_{\\sqrt{π}}^{\\sqrt{2π}}∫_{0}^{y^{2}}\\frac{\\sin y^{2}}{y}dxdy &= ∫_{\\sqrt{π}}^{\\sqrt{2π}}\\frac{\\sin y^{2}}{y}[∫_{0}^{y^{2}}dx]dy \\\\\n&= ∫_{\\sqrt{π}}^{\\sqrt{2π}}\\frac{\\sin y^{2}}{y}[x]_{0}^{y^{2}}dy \\\\\n&= ∫_{\\sqrt{π}}^{\\sqrt{2π}}\\frac{\\sin y^{2}}{y}(y^{2} - 0)dy \\\\\n&= ∫_{\\sqrt{π}}^{\\sqrt{2π}}y\\sin y^{2}dy\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_{\\sqrt{π}}^{\\sqrt{2π}}y\\sin y^{2}dy &= [- \\frac{1}{2}\\cos y^{2}]_{\\sqrt{π}}^{\\sqrt{2π}} \\\\\n&=-\\frac{1}{2}(\\cos 2π - \\cos π) \\\\\n&=-1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535130 false] [17592186133578 1145 "The integral is defined over a type II region.\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $x$, treating $y$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{\\pi }}^{\\sqrt{2\\pi }}{\\int }_{0}^{{y}^{2}}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡{y}^{2}}{y}\\,dx\\,dy & ={\\int }_{\\sqrt{\\pi }}^{\\sqrt{2\\pi }}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡{y}^{2}}{y}[{\\int }_{0}^{{y}^{2}}dx]\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\sqrt{\\pi }}^{\\sqrt{2\\pi }}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡{y}^{2}}{y}[x]_{0}^{{y}^{2}}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\sqrt{\\pi }}^{\\sqrt{2\\pi }}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡{y}^{2}}{y}({y}^{2}-0)\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\sqrt{\\pi }}^{\\sqrt{2\\pi }}y\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡{y}^{2}\\,dy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $y\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{\\pi }}^{\\sqrt{2\\pi }}y\\operatorname{sin}⁡{y}^{2}\\,dy & ={[-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡{y}^{2}]}_{\\sqrt{\\pi }}^{\\sqrt{2\\pi }} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\pi -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi ) \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535130 true] [17592186133579 1100 "Evaluate the double integral $\\displaystyle \\iint \\limits_{D} xy^2\\,\\mathrm{d}A,\\:$\nwhere $D$ is the region enclosed between the curves $x^2+y^2=4$ and $x=0$, and above the line $y=-1$, as shown in the picture.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2152/Source/Images/d3efc009081220a488deb11b6d536fce.png)" 13194139535130 false] [17592186133579 1100 "Evaluate the double integral $\\displaystyle \\iint \\limits_{D} xy^2\\,\\mathrm{d}A,\\:$\nwhere $D$ is the region enclosed between the curves $x^2+y^2=4$ and $x=0,$ and above the line $y=-1,$ as shown in the picture.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055472/assets/f93aeee59ad97fde0efceff342e32a19062187261abec865616bfa963f1084a6.png)" 13194139535130 true] [17592186133579 1145 "Notice that $D$ is a type II region. Since\n\n$$\nx \\ge 0\n$$\n\nwe obtain the following equations in terms of $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x^{2} + y^{2} = 4 & ⟹x = \\sqrt{4 - y^{2}} \\\\ x = 0 & ⟹x = 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNow, let's determine the limits of integration.\n\n- To determine the $y$-limits, we need to find the $y$-coordinates of the points at which the curves intersect. So, we set the following equation and solve it for $y$:\n$\\sqrt{4 - y^{2}}|= 0; 4 - y^{2}|= 0; y^{2}|= 4; y|= ± 2$.\nSo, since our region lies above the line $y=-1$, the $y$-limits are\n$-1 \\le y \\le 2$.\n- To determine the left and right functions, we draw a horizontal arrow from left to right through the region, as usual.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2152/Source/Images/8b3b3ffed1e4aeb6dd4c9e503c34687d.png)\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\nD = \\left\\{(x,y) \\:: \\: -1 \\leq y \\leq 2, \\quad 0 \\leq x \\leq \\sqrt{4-y^2} \\right\\}\n$$\n\nSo, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\iint \\limits_{D} xy^2\\,\\mathrm{d}A = \\int_{-1}^2 \\int_0^{\\sqrt{4-y^2}}\\, xy^2\\, \\mathrm dx\\, \\mathrm{d} y\n$$\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $x$, treating $y$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_{-1}^{2}∫_{0}^{\\sqrt{4 - y^{2}}}xy^{2}dxdy &= ∫_{-1}^{2}y^{2}[∫_{0}^{\\sqrt{4 - y^{2}}}xdx]dy \\\\\n&= ∫_{-1}^{2}y^{2}[\\frac{x^{2}}{2}]_{0}^{\\sqrt{4 - y^{2}}}dy \\\\\n&= ∫_{-1}^{2}\\frac{y^{2}}{2}(4 - y^{2} - 0)dy \\\\\n&= ∫_{-1}^{2}(2y^{2} - \\frac{y^{4}}{2})dy\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_{-1}^{2}(2y^{2} - \\frac{y^{4}}{2})dy &= [\\frac{2}{3}y^{3} - \\frac{y^{5}}{10}]_{-1}^{2} \\\\\n&= (\\frac{16}{3} - \\frac{32}{10}) - (-\\frac{2}{3} + \\frac{1}{10}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{27}{10}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535130 false] [17592186133579 1145 "Notice that $D$ is a type II region. Since $x \\ge 0,$ we obtain the following equations in terms of $y\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4 & \\,⟹\\,x=\\sqrt{4-{y}^{2}} \\\\ x=0 & \\,⟹\\,x=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's determine the limits of integration.\n\n- To determine the $y$-limits, we need to find the $y$-coordinates of the points at which the curves intersect. So, we set the following equation and solve it for $y\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{4-{y}^{2}} & =0 \\\\ 4-{y}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {y}^{2} & =4 \\\\ y & =\\pm 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, since our region lies above the line $y=-1,$ the $y$-limits are \n$-1 \\le y \\le 2.$\n\n- To determine the left and right functions, we draw a horizontal arrow from left to right through the region, as usual.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055472/assets/8d77e3caa18191710e056b72ea54e5820e6244d9e90719222deed409d3992c5b.png)\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\nD = \\left\\{ (x,y) \\: : \\: -1 \\leq y \\leq 2, \\quad 0 \\leq x  \\leq \\sqrt{4-y^2} \\right\\}.\n$$\n\nSo, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\iint \\limits_{D} xy^2\\,\\mathrm{d}A = \\int_{-1}^2 \\int_0^{\\sqrt{4-y^2}}\\, xy^2\\, \\mathrm dx\\,   \\mathrm{d}  y\n$$\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $x$, treating $y$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{4-{y}^{2}}}\\,x{y}^{2}\\,dx\\,dy & ={\\int }_{-1}^{2}{y}^{2}[{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{4-{y}^{2}}}x\\,dx]\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{2}{y}^{2}{[\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{\\sqrt{4-{y}^{2}}}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{2}\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}(4-{y}^{2}-0)\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{2}(2{y}^{2}-\\frac{{y}^{4}}{2})\\,dy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $y\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{2}(2{y}^{2}-\\frac{{y}^{4}}{2})\\,dy & ={[\\frac{2}{3}{y}^{3}-\\frac{{y}^{5}}{10}]}_{-1}^{2} \\\\  & =(\\frac{16}{3}-\\frac{32}{10})-(-\\frac{2}{3}+\\frac{1}{10}) \\\\  & =\\frac{27}{10}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535130 true] [17592186133761 1020 #edb/ref 17592186332695 13194139535130 true] [17592186133761 1020 #edb/ref 17592186332765 13194139535130 true] [17592186133761 1020 #edb/ref 17592186332772 13194139535130 true] [17592186133761 1020 #edb/ref 17592186332779 13194139535130 true] [17592186133762 1020 #edb/ref 17592186332709 13194139535130 true] [17592186133762 1020 #edb/ref 17592186332716 13194139535130 true] [17592186133762 1020 #edb/ref 17592186332723 13194139535130 true] [17592186133762 1020 #edb/ref 17592186332786 13194139535130 true] [17592186133763 1020 #edb/ref 17592186332702 13194139535130 true] [17592186133763 1020 #edb/ref 17592186332751 13194139535130 true] [17592186133763 1020 #edb/ref 17592186332793 13194139535130 true] [17592186133763 1020 #edb/ref 17592186332800 13194139535130 true] [17592186133763 1020 #edb/ref 17592186332807 13194139535130 true] [17592186133764 1020 #edb/ref 17592186332730 13194139535130 true] [17592186133764 1020 #edb/ref 17592186332737 13194139535130 true] [17592186133764 1020 #edb/ref 17592186332744 13194139535130 true] [17592186133764 1020 #edb/ref 17592186332758 13194139535130 true] [17592186133764 1020 #edb/ref 17592186332814 13194139535130 true] [17592186134222 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535130 true] [17592186134222 1100 "Consider the finite region $D$ in the $xy$-plane enclosed between the lines $y = 2x$ and $y =-x$ from $y = 0$ to $y = 4$. What is $∬_(D)(x^{2} + y^{2})dA$ expressed as a repeated integral?" 13194139535130 false] [17592186134222 1100 "Consider the finite region $D$ in the $xy$-plane enclosed between the lines $y=2x$ and $y=-x$ from $y=0$ to $y=4.$ What is ${∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,dA$ expressed as a repeated integral?" 13194139535130 true] [17592186134222 1145 "First, let's represent $D$ as a type II region. Writing $y=2x$ and $y=-x$ in terms of $y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y=2x & \\,⟹\\,x=\\frac{x}{2} \\\\ y=-x & \\,⟹\\,x=-y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine the left and right functions, we draw a horizontal arrow from left to right through the region, as usual.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055472/assets/df512140098a6fc6adc03e4d98199896cf15ed1cdd40578aea14ff1e83dfeba3.png)\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 4,\\,-y\\le x\\le \\frac{y}{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,dA & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{-y}^{y/2}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,dx\\,dy\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535130 true] [17592186134223 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535130 true] [17592186134223 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055472/assets/c752eb32fc94ae630ef3998c81a6b9b0af5c222a367f87db8bbacb877716c16c.png)\n\nIf $D$ is the finite region enclosed by the lines $x=2-y,\\,$ $x=\\frac{y}{2},$ and $y=0,$ as shown above, then ${∬}_{D}xy\\,dA=$" 13194139535130 true] [17592186134223 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2152/Source/Images/q-40596.png)\n\nIf $D$ is the finite region enclosed by the lines $x = 2 - y$, $x = \\frac{y}{2}$, and $y = 0$, as shown above, then $∬_(D)xydA =$" 13194139535130 false] [17592186134223 1145 "First, let's represent $D$ as a type II region. \n\n- To determine the $y$-limits, we need to find the $y$-coordinates of the points at which the curves intersect. So, we set the following equation and solve it for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2-y & =\\frac{y}{2} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{2}y & =-2 \\\\ y & =\\frac{4}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $y$-limits are \n$0\\le y\\le \\frac{4}{3}.$\n\n- To determine the left and right functions, we draw a horizontal arrow from left to right through the region, as usual.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055472/assets/e6c9332f8f59e2561af9ba5aa89311263fb772dc987ed0b15d91a6d5eea5cf8e.png)\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le \\frac{4}{3},\\,\\frac{y}{2}\\le x\\le 2-y}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}xy\\,dA & ={\\int }_{0}^{4/3}{\\int }_{y/2}^{2-y}xy\\,dx\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4/3}[{\\int }_{y/2}^{2-y}xy\\,dx]dy\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535130 true] [17592186332695 1017 #edb/ref 17592186332696 13194139535130 true] [17592186332695 1089 "q-132402" 13194139535130 true] [17592186332695 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535130 true] [17592186332695 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055472/assets/bfa591e51f42e8e52b67e61c23b02ea69b03bdecc1c4b815370ce6b2b0b5f213.png)\n\nIf $D$ is the finite region enclosed by the $x$-axis and the curves $x={y}^{2}-4$ and $x=4-{y}^{2},$ as shown above, then ${∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA=$" 13194139535130 true] [17592186332695 1113 #uuid "19602799-cf55-5cdc-a41d-4ab0cc8e92dc" 13194139535130 true] [17592186332695 1145 "First, let's represent $D$ as a type II region. To determine the left and right functions, we draw a horizontal arrow from left to right through the region, as usual.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055472/assets/85d3faac9a27353a8ea5151b3a32f1065aecb02fd07e0b20727c35de0a7fb216.png)\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 2,\\,{y}^{2}-4\\le x\\le 4-{y}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{{y}^{2}-4}^{4-{y}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,dx\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}[{\\int }_{{y}^{2}-4}^{4-{y}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,dx]dy\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535130 true] [17592186332696 1000 #edb/ref 17592186332697 13194139535130 true] [17592186332696 1000 #edb/ref 17592186332698 13194139535130 true] [17592186332696 1000 #edb/ref 17592186332699 13194139535130 true] [17592186332696 1000 #edb/ref 17592186332700 13194139535130 true] [17592186332696 1000 #edb/ref 17592186332701 13194139535130 true] [17592186332696 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535130 true] [17592186332696 1041 "selection" 13194139535130 true] [17592186332696 1104 #edb/ref 17592186332698 13194139535130 true] [17592186332696 1110 #uuid "7fad1b46-1023-5aa2-9466-d27d00ead44f" 13194139535130 true] [17592186332697 1034 "{\\int }_{0}^{2}[{\\int }_{4-{y}^{2}}^{{y}^{2}-4}f(x,y)\\,dx]dy" 13194139535130 true] [17592186332697 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535130 true] [17592186332697 1111 #uuid "4cb22083-7269-5829-8376-2d34d691e5c2" 13194139535130 true] [17592186332698 1034 "{\\int }_{0}^{2}[{\\int }_{{y}^{2}-4}^{4-{y}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,dx]dy" 13194139535130 true] [17592186332698 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535130 true] [17592186332698 1111 #uuid "a3dda242-850d-5efd-a9c3-d41fca498194" 13194139535130 true] [17592186332699 1034 "{\\int }_{{y}^{2}-4}^{4-{y}^{2}}[{\\int }_{0}^{2}f(x,y)\\,dx]dy" 13194139535130 true] [17592186332699 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535130 true] [17592186332699 1111 #uuid "aefb590b-1954-51e6-82ed-f44cbd2d9133" 13194139535130 true] [17592186332700 1034 "{\\int }_{{y}^{2}-4}^{2}[{\\int }_{0}^{4-{y}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,dx]dy" 13194139535130 true] [17592186332700 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535130 true] [17592186332700 1111 #uuid "48b83e21-4bc3-5cff-856f-a3dd79430ec3" 13194139535130 true] [17592186332701 1034 "{\\int }_{0}^{4-{y}^{2}}[{\\int }_{{y}^{2}-4}^{2}f(x,y)\\,dx]dy" 13194139535130 true] [17592186332701 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535130 true] [17592186332701 1111 #uuid "57ced348-cbd8-5e7e-9e49-f9ec2640e0b8" 13194139535130 true] [17592186332702 1017 #edb/ref 17592186332703 13194139535130 true] [17592186332702 1089 "q-132421" 13194139535130 true] [17592186332702 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535130 true] [17592186332702 1100 "Calculate ${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡y}{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡y}\\,dx\\,dy.$" 13194139535130 true] [17592186332702 1113 #uuid "30f6dbff-f649-5725-b4bd-0bb3cfdf4187" 13194139535130 true] [17592186332702 1145 "The integral is defined over a type II region.\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $x$, treating $y$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡y}{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡y}\\,dx\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡y}[{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡y}dx]dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡y}[x]_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡y}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡y}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡y-0)\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡y}\\,dy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡y}\\,dy & =[-{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡y}]_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =-{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi }-(-{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡0}) \\\\  & =-{e}^{-1}+{e}^{1} \\\\  & =e-\\frac{1}{e}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535130 true] [17592186332703 1000 #edb/ref 17592186332704 13194139535130 true] [17592186332703 1000 #edb/ref 17592186332705 13194139535130 true] [17592186332703 1000 #edb/ref 17592186332706 13194139535130 true] [17592186332703 1000 #edb/ref 17592186332707 13194139535130 true] [17592186332703 1000 #edb/ref 17592186332708 13194139535130 true] [17592186332703 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535130 true] [17592186332703 1041 "selection" 13194139535130 true] [17592186332703 1104 #edb/ref 17592186332708 13194139535130 true] [17592186332703 1110 #uuid "26965548-0d00-5094-aca1-d03a9c036f40" 13194139535130 true] [17592186332704 1034 "1+\\frac{1}{e}" 13194139535130 true] [17592186332704 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535130 true] [17592186332704 1111 #uuid "d78e9ee2-047b-5e89-b606-60fc204aadad" 13194139535130 true] [17592186332705 1034 "e+1" 13194139535130 true] [17592186332705 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535130 true] [17592186332705 1111 #uuid "6c759950-3a42-51cc-993b-ab64b895ecc5" 13194139535130 true] [17592186332706 1034 "1-\\frac{1}{e}" 13194139535130 true] [17592186332706 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535130 true] [17592186332706 1111 #uuid "3fe6c62d-6e38-58fd-b085-86453a09e5bd" 13194139535130 true] [17592186332707 1034 "e-1" 13194139535130 true] [17592186332707 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535130 true] [17592186332707 1111 #uuid "9964b396-9653-52ce-9579-6cadd757d39b" 13194139535130 true] [17592186332708 1034 "e-\\frac{1}{e}" 13194139535130 true] [17592186332708 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535130 true] [17592186332708 1111 #uuid "58103bfa-a285-51e1-a2be-f5e7c605dc15" 13194139535130 true] [17592186332709 1017 #edb/ref 17592186332710 13194139535130 true] [17592186332709 1089 "q-132499" 13194139535130 true] [17592186332709 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535130 true] [17592186332709 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055472/assets/6d6597bff5d28155e9a40f953b0ab0652f175e4d4d0aaf6f28c50e6d5bc907d2.png)\n\nIf $D$ is the finite region enclosed by the lines $x=y-1,\\,$ $x=5-\\frac{y}{2},$ and $y=1,$ as shown above, then ${∬}_{D}x\\sqrt{y}\\,dA=$" 13194139535130 true] [17592186332709 1113 #uuid "0ea00186-dbdd-5fef-968b-f1e2bc005692" 13194139535130 true] [17592186332709 1145 "First, let's represent $D$ as a type II region. \n\n- To determine the $y$-limits, we need to find the $y$-coordinates of the points at which the curves intersect. So, we set the following equation and solve it for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-1 & =-\\frac{y}{2}+5 \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}y & =6 \\\\ y & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $y$-limits are \n$1\\le y\\le 4.$\n\n- To determine the left and right functions, we draw a horizontal arrow from left to right through the region, as usual.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055472/assets/e2de1b62c6f3d0a15c0b95e3204f7889f6fc1ffbe108bdba7871c150091f6977.png)\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le y\\le 4,\\,y-1\\le x\\le 5-\\frac{y}{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}x\\sqrt{y}\\,dA & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}{\\int }_{y-1}^{5-y/2}x\\sqrt{y}\\,dx\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}[{\\int }_{y-1}^{5-y/2}x\\sqrt{y}\\,dx]dy\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535130 true] [17592186332710 1000 #edb/ref 17592186332711 13194139535130 true] [17592186332710 1000 #edb/ref 17592186332712 13194139535130 true] [17592186332710 1000 #edb/ref 17592186332713 13194139535130 true] [17592186332710 1000 #edb/ref 17592186332714 13194139535130 true] [17592186332710 1000 #edb/ref 17592186332715 13194139535130 true] [17592186332710 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535130 true] [17592186332710 1041 "selection" 13194139535130 true] [17592186332710 1104 #edb/ref 17592186332713 13194139535130 true] [17592186332710 1110 #uuid "5cbdac7b-e62c-5341-bf37-c98789712411" 13194139535130 true] [17592186332711 1034 "{\\int }_{1}^{5-y/2}[{\\int }_{y-1}^{4}x\\sqrt{y}\\,dx]dy" 13194139535130 true] [17592186332711 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535130 true] [17592186332711 1111 #uuid "b7dd10a0-cc46-5b59-87d3-9174e3a74ef6" 13194139535130 true] [17592186332712 1034 "{\\int }_{y-1}^{4}[{\\int }_{1}^{5-y/2}x\\sqrt{y}\\,dx]dy" 13194139535130 true] [17592186332712 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535130 true] [17592186332712 1111 #uuid "9c9d2bce-761c-57cb-bed7-5eabbdc8ee3c" 13194139535130 true] [17592186332713 1034 "{\\int }_{1}^{4}[{\\int }_{y-1}^{5-y/2}x\\sqrt{y}\\,dx]dy" 13194139535130 true] [17592186332713 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535130 true] [17592186332713 1111 #uuid "ed7083eb-ec17-5ebc-8585-9a22c4d3bbf8" 13194139535130 true] [17592186332714 1034 "{\\int }_{y-1}^{5-y/2}[{\\int }_{1}^{4}x\\sqrt{y}\\,dx]dy" 13194139535130 true] [17592186332714 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535130 true] [17592186332714 1111 #uuid "cae9b901-9f03-5cca-92e8-1f6e00cc8cca" 13194139535130 true] [17592186332715 1034 "{\\int }_{1}^{4}[{\\int }_{5-y/2}^{y-1}x\\sqrt{y}\\,dx]dy" 13194139535130 true] [17592186332715 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535130 true] [17592186332715 1111 #uuid "6ed45d05-79e5-542d-9b49-2d95fe7a5a8c" 13194139535130 true] [17592186332716 1017 #edb/ref 17592186332717 13194139535130 true] [17592186332716 1089 "q-132510" 13194139535130 true] [17592186332716 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535130 true] [17592186332716 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055472/assets/f390e83f737b3b20931df5e97a9ea0750b8b190acba14d1cd963aaab5175ba50.png)\n\nIf $D$ is the finite region enclosed between the curves $x=\\frac{1}{2}(y-2)^{2}-2$ and $x=2-{y}^{2},$ above the $x$-axis, as shown above, then ${∬}_{D}x{y}^{2}\\,dA=$" 13194139535130 true] [17592186332716 1113 #uuid "11862ce2-c090-5636-9ec3-c43c9808c1ec" 13194139535130 true] [17592186332716 1145 "First, let's represent $D$ as a type II region. \n\n- To determine the $y$-limits, we need to find the $y$-coordinates of the points at which the curves intersect. So, we set the following equation and solve it for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}(y-2)^{2}-2 & =2-{y}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}({y}^{2}-4y+4)+{y}^{2}-4 & =0 \\\\ {y}^{2}-4y+4+2{y}^{2}-8 & =0 \\\\ 3{y}^{2}-4y-4 & =0 \\\\ (3y+2)(y-2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, since our region lies above the line $y=0,$ the $y$-limits are \n$0\\le y\\le 2.$,\n\n- To determine the left and right functions, we draw a horizontal arrow from left to right through the region, as usual.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055472/assets/1b67c65c78dc847c2bf4bf53ad67f770a1406570548497edd55939c3a68a4c5c.png)\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 2,\\,\\frac{1}{2}(y-2)^{2}-2\\le x\\le 2-{y}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}x{y}^{2}\\,dA & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{(y-2)^{2}/2-2}^{2-{y}^{2}}x{y}^{2}\\,dx\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}[{\\int }_{(y-2)^{2}/2-2}^{2-{y}^{2}}x{y}^{2}\\,dx]dy\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535130 true] [17592186332717 1000 #edb/ref 17592186332718 13194139535130 true] [17592186332717 1000 #edb/ref 17592186332719 13194139535130 true] [17592186332717 1000 #edb/ref 17592186332720 13194139535130 true] [17592186332717 1000 #edb/ref 17592186332721 13194139535130 true] [17592186332717 1000 #edb/ref 17592186332722 13194139535130 true] [17592186332717 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535130 true] [17592186332717 1041 "selection" 13194139535130 true] [17592186332717 1104 #edb/ref 17592186332722 